<entry><title>Virginia Capital Trail, November 6</title><id>https://rvabikefeed.github.io/post/95f56c657648fd1c</id><updated>2026-11-06T14:33:34Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T14:33:34Z</published><author><name>Virginia Capital Trail</name></author><content type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;From Virginia Capital Trail&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;⚠️ TRAIL ALERT Update: The water has receded! There is still a little mud and residue left behind, so please stay...&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://rvabikefeed.github.io/images/63c06c5466fc875d.jpg&quot;/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;⚠️ TRAIL ALERT Update: The water has receded! There is still a little mud and residue left behind, so please stay...&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://rvabikefeed.github.io/images/708f83a827ad1da6.jpg&quot;/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;⚠️ TRAIL ALERT Update: The water has receded! There is still a little mud and residue left behind, so please stay alert, watch your step, and proceed slowly. Stay safe!&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
Flooding has been reported on the Capital Trail just west of Four Mile Creek Trailhead, near MM 41, where I-295 crosses the Trail. This section is between Four Mile Creek Park and Doran Road.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
Other sections of the Capital Trail may also have standing water, mud, and debris following today’s storm. Please use extra caution and avoid the flooded section.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
